# Jira

- Default project key: **US** (URL Shortener)
- Create tickets as **Task** unless the user asks for Bug or Story
- Default priority is **Normal**. Use High or Low only when the user says so
- Do not ask which project to use unless the user names a different one
- Use the official Atlassian MCP (OAuth). Never put API tokens, emails, or passwords in tickets, rules, or commits.
- Never include Jira issue URLs (browse links, `atlassian.net/browse/…`, markdown links to tickets). Issue keys like `US-5` are fine as plain text.

## README issues

[`README.md`](README.md) has an **Issues to fix** section. When you **update** a Jira ticket, if that same issue is already listed there, update that README entry so it stays aligned (status, recommended fix, scope). If it is **not** already in the README, do not add it and do not change the Issues section. Do not put issue URLs in the README.

## Description format

Write descriptions as Markdown and save them as Atlassian document format (official MCP `contentFormat: markdown`). Jira Cloud does not treat `**Details**` as a heading; that shows up as literal asterisks.

- Section titles are `###` headings (not `##`, not bold-only lines)
- Standard sections: Details, Background, Scope, Accepted Criteria, Out of scope, Notes
- Omit a section if it has nothing useful
- After create, return the issue key only (no browse URL)

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD: bold-only "headers" stay as **Details** -->
**Details**
Add rate limiting.

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
### Details

Redirects are the hot path. Unbounded `GET /urls/:code` can overwhelm the replica.

### Accepted Criteria

- Over-limit responses return 429 JSON and a Retry-After header
- Create traffic is not blocked by the redirect limiter
```
